1
00:00:09,095 --> 00:00:15,439
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'd like to read from some talks from Ajahn Chah, recorded talks, transcribed talks.

2
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:24,603
[SPEAKER_02]: This one is called, about being careful, and it can be found on the internet, about being careful.

3
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:34,208
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha taught to see the body in the body.

4
00:00:35,489 --> 00:00:36,349
[SPEAKER_02]: What does this mean?

5
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:42,253
[SPEAKER_02]: We are all familiar with the parts of the body such as hair, nails, teeth and skin.

6
00:00:43,294 --> 00:00:45,135
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how do we see the body in the body?

7
00:00:46,796 --> 00:00:55,742
[SPEAKER_02]: If we recognize all these things as being impermanent, unsatisfactory and not-self, that is what is called seeing the body in the body.

8
00:00:57,685 --> 00:01:02,969
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it isn't necessary to go into detail and meditate on separate... on the separate parts.

9
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:05,551
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like having fruit in a basket.

10
00:01:06,712 --> 00:01:10,355
[SPEAKER_02]: If we have already counted the pieces of fruit, then we know what's there.

11
00:01:11,056 --> 00:01:17,661
[SPEAKER_02]: And when we need to, we can pick up the basket and take it away, and all the pieces come with it.

12
00:01:18,642 --> 00:01:21,745
[SPEAKER_02]: We know the fruits are all there, so we don't have to count it again.

13
00:01:24,227 --> 00:01:39,579
[SPEAKER_02]: Having meditated on the 32 parts of the body and recognized them as something not stable or permanent, we no longer need to worry ourselves separating them like this and meditating in such detail.

14
00:01:39,619 --> 00:01:44,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Just as with the basket of fruit, we don't have to dump all the fruit out and count it again and again.

15
00:01:46,023 --> 00:01:53,229
[SPEAKER_02]: But we do carry the basket along to our destination, walking mindfully and carefully, taking care not to stumble and fall.

16
00:01:58,108 --> 00:02:10,730
[SPEAKER_02]: When we see the body in the body, which means we see the dhamma in the body, knowing our own and others' bodies as impermanent phenomena, then we don't need detailed explanations.

17
00:02:11,871 --> 00:02:17,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Sitting here, we have mindfulness constantly in control, knowing things as they are.

18
00:02:18,572 --> 00:02:20,412
[SPEAKER_02]: And meditation then becomes quite simple.

19
00:02:21,852 --> 00:02:24,493
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same if we meditate on the word Buddha,

20
00:02:25,894 --> 00:02:31,378
[SPEAKER_02]: If we understand what Buddha really is, then we don't need to repeat the word Buddha.

21
00:02:32,058 --> 00:02:34,540
[SPEAKER_02]: It means having full knowledge and firm awareness.

22
00:02:35,481 --> 00:02:36,441
[SPEAKER_02]: This is meditation.

23
00:02:40,124 --> 00:02:46,088
[SPEAKER_02]: Having the inner sensitivity that is always aware of right and wrong is called Buddha.

24
00:02:47,849 --> 00:02:51,252
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not necessary to be continually repeating Buddha.

25
00:02:52,955 --> 00:02:54,596
[SPEAKER_02]: You've counted the fruit in your basket.

26
00:02:55,797 --> 00:03:01,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Every time you sit down, you don't have to go to the trouble of spilling out the fruit and counting it again.

27
00:03:03,163 --> 00:03:04,344
[SPEAKER_02]: You can leave it in the basket.

28
00:03:05,507 --> 00:03:08,610
[SPEAKER_02]: But someone with mistaken attachment will keep counting.

29
00:03:09,330 --> 00:03:13,634
[SPEAKER_02]: He'll stop under a tree, spill it out and count, and put it back in the basket.

30
00:03:14,415 --> 00:03:18,058
[SPEAKER_02]: Then he'll walk onto the next stopping place and do it again.

31
00:03:18,738 --> 00:03:20,240
[SPEAKER_02]: But he's just counting the same fruit.

32
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:22,181
[SPEAKER_02]: This is craving itself.

33
00:03:23,062 --> 00:03:26,865
[SPEAKER_02]: He's afraid that if he doesn't count, there'll be some mistake.

34
00:03:26,905 --> 00:03:29,588
[SPEAKER_02]: We are afraid that if we don't keep saying Buddha, we'll be mistaken.

35
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:31,600
[SPEAKER_02]: How are we mistaken?

36
00:03:32,601 --> 00:03:36,802
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only the person who doesn't know how much fruit there is, who needs to count.

37
00:03:37,702 --> 00:03:42,363
[SPEAKER_02]: Once you know, you can take it easy and just leave it in the basket.

38
00:03:42,383 --> 00:03:43,943
[SPEAKER_02]: When you're sitting, you just sit.

39
00:03:44,463 --> 00:03:46,304
[SPEAKER_02]: When you're lying down, you just lie down.

40
00:03:46,884 --> 00:03:50,185
[SPEAKER_02]: Because your fruit is all there with you.

41
00:03:50,285 --> 00:03:54,866
[SPEAKER_02]: It's one with everyone.

42
00:03:56,306 --> 00:03:56,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the same.

43
00:03:57,467 --> 00:03:58,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, go for it.

44
00:04:00,388 --> 00:04:04,532
[SPEAKER_02]: We should see all the forms of craving which cause us to have desires.

45
00:04:05,432 --> 00:04:07,995
[SPEAKER_02]: But talking about desire can be confusing.

46
00:04:08,115 --> 00:04:16,602
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people get the idea that any kind of desire, such as desire for food and for material requisites, for life, is craving.

47
00:04:17,463 --> 00:04:21,006
[SPEAKER_02]: But we can have this kind of desire in an ordinary and natural way.

48
00:04:21,747 --> 00:04:25,230
[SPEAKER_02]: When you're hungry and desire food, you can take a meal and be done with it.

49
00:04:26,102 --> 00:04:27,082
[SPEAKER_02]: That's quite ordinary.

50
00:04:27,863 --> 00:04:29,824
[SPEAKER_02]: This is the desire that's within boundaries.

51
00:04:30,164 --> 00:04:32,765
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't have ill effects.

52
00:04:33,565 --> 00:04:35,946
[SPEAKER_02]: This kind of desire isn't sensuality.

53
00:04:36,826 --> 00:04:40,408
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's sensuality, then it becomes something more than desire.

54
00:04:40,428 --> 00:04:50,112
[SPEAKER_02]: There'll be cravings for more things to consume, seeking out flavors, seeking enjoyment, in ways that bring hardship and trouble, such as drinking liquor and beer.

55
00:04:59,430 --> 00:05:02,172
[SPEAKER_02]: So, those are excerpts from the talk.

56
00:05:05,075 --> 00:05:07,837
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are excerpts from the talk about being careful.

57
00:05:08,958 --> 00:05:12,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can see, I mean, you could just read that as an inspirational story.

58
00:05:12,981 --> 00:05:23,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, just be mindful and whatnot, which is usually how I would hear people talk about Ajahn Chah, but you could be quite strict about things he's saying.

59
00:05:24,130 --> 00:05:25,271
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see...

60
00:05:25,311 --> 00:05:26,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so what is in common?

61
00:05:28,084 --> 00:05:33,590
[SPEAKER_00]: What is in common to not constantly counting the parts of your body, not constantly reviewing your body?

62
00:05:34,070 --> 00:05:40,517
[SPEAKER_00]: What is in common to not constantly repeating buddho, buddho, buddho, or touching, touching, moving, moving, sitting, sitting, hair, nails, skin?

63
00:05:41,058 --> 00:05:42,059
[SPEAKER_00]: What is in common to that?

64
00:05:42,479 --> 00:05:47,645
[SPEAKER_00]: The common is establishing your mind upon the context, which he refers to as basket.

65
00:05:48,807 --> 00:05:56,152
[SPEAKER_00]: When you know you have this in your basket, you don't need to keep taking it out and reviewing it, which is usually what most people's meditation practice is.

66
00:05:56,713 --> 00:06:03,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keep doing it robotically, methodically, and and completely losing the sight of the context on account of it.

67
00:06:03,778 --> 00:06:04,478
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm talking about.

68
00:06:04,498 --> 00:06:13,685
[SPEAKER_00]: The peripheral awareness is basically becoming aware of the context, whereby you don't have to keep reminding yourself what you're doing, because you already know what you're doing.

69
00:06:14,625 --> 00:06:16,166
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what he's saying there.

70
00:06:16,546 --> 00:06:18,107
[SPEAKER_00]: You already have the basket.

71
00:06:18,367 --> 00:06:21,289
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to keep stopping and chucking the fruit out to count it again.

72
00:06:22,389 --> 00:06:29,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless, your mind is overwhelmed by your own doubt, lack of clarity, lack of context, craving, as he said himself.

73
00:06:30,578 --> 00:06:31,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And same when people do the mantra.

74
00:06:31,979 --> 00:06:44,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, yeah, you can bring to your mind, buddho, buddho, if that makes you contemplate the quality of the Buddha, the quality of renunciation, the quality of freedom from suffering, and then discern that context, and that's how you practice buddho.

75
00:06:45,232 --> 00:06:54,381
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're just going like a like a parrot to yourself, just buddho, buddho, buddho, expecting that verbal repetition to result in some sort of samādhi, well, that's extremely wrong samādhi.

76
00:06:55,888 --> 00:07:09,172
[SPEAKER_00]: And same, if you result... if you're expecting experience to arise on that of you just... on account of you just constantly counting your fruit and not developing knowledge of the basket that contains all the fruit, means that's not the samādhi the Buddha was teaching.

77
00:07:13,113 --> 00:07:16,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people can not look into this and then just sort of... ah, okay, you don't need to...

78
00:07:17,812 --> 00:07:18,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Look at anything.

79
00:07:18,472 --> 00:07:18,632
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

80
00:07:19,192 --> 00:07:20,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Count the fruit first time.

81
00:07:20,753 --> 00:07:33,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Review your body, as in, bring in the context of a body that's subjected to sickness, aging and death, of a body that's not in your control, of a body that is subjected to elements.

82
00:07:34,537 --> 00:07:39,679
[SPEAKER_00]: And if reviewing parts of the body will enable you to discern that context initially, that's fine.

83
00:07:39,859 --> 00:07:41,659
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would have listed those things.

84
00:07:42,279 --> 00:07:46,861
[SPEAKER_00]: But not as a sort of blind, methodical, mechanical repetition practice.

85
00:07:47,795 --> 00:07:50,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby you don't take responsibility or discernment of the context.

86
00:07:51,837 --> 00:08:00,222
[SPEAKER_00]: And then exactly, as Ajahn Chah describes, if you do it rightly, if you discern this context peripherally, it's very simple thing to do then, to practice.

87
00:08:00,282 --> 00:08:01,462
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to keep bringing it up.

88
00:08:01,742 --> 00:08:03,663
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to be wondering, what do I do next?

89
00:08:04,824 --> 00:08:09,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no next within the mind established upon the general context, the right context.

90
00:08:10,627 --> 00:08:11,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you would exactly know,

91
00:08:12,649 --> 00:08:14,391
[SPEAKER_00]: This is within the context.

92
00:08:14,671 --> 00:08:16,072
[SPEAKER_00]: This is against the context.

93
00:08:16,652 --> 00:08:17,753
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, this is wholesome.

94
00:08:18,074 --> 00:08:18,934
[SPEAKER_00]: This is unwholesome.

95
00:08:19,535 --> 00:08:20,536
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all you need to know.

96
00:08:21,577 --> 00:08:23,999
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the last paragraph you mentioned.

97
00:08:24,039 --> 00:08:26,561
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, yeah, people have no sense of context.

98
00:08:27,382 --> 00:08:28,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is a problem.

99
00:08:30,384 --> 00:08:33,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, well, that's just craving, isn't it?

100
00:08:33,267 --> 00:08:34,428
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to sit on a nice chair.

101
00:08:34,448 --> 00:08:35,228
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just craving.

102
00:08:35,288 --> 00:08:36,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is craving.

103
00:08:36,750 --> 00:08:37,110
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't.

104
00:08:38,147 --> 00:08:41,510
[SPEAKER_00]: There is craving that leads to more craving, and there is craving that leads to the end of craving.

105
00:08:42,091 --> 00:08:43,813
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should crave renunciation.

106
00:08:43,893 --> 00:08:45,014
[SPEAKER_00]: You should crave solitude.

107
00:08:46,135 --> 00:08:50,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, crave for those things, because they will result in complete understanding of the nature of craving.

108
00:08:51,861 --> 00:08:56,085
[SPEAKER_00]: You should crave the ability to be able to withstand the elements, the hardship.

109
00:08:57,945 --> 00:09:08,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like apply the blanket, oh, that's all craving, failing to see that that's your craving at the time, because of which you don't make any effort towards abandoning the craving, just dismissing it, because it's all craving.

110
00:09:09,708 --> 00:09:10,809
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Ajahn Chah describes.

111
00:09:11,169 --> 00:09:13,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those desires are not sensuality.

112
00:09:13,829 --> 00:09:17,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is clearly unwholesome, intentional and unwholesome.

113
00:09:18,191 --> 00:09:22,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can keep refining those desires, but the point is to know where the line is.

114
00:09:23,918 --> 00:09:30,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And not just go around mechanically doing your mantras and vipassana techniques, and mechanically denying everything.

115
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everything is craving, everything is impermanent, everything is not myself.

116
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:34,461
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not true.

117
00:09:37,621 --> 00:09:41,802
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said things are on account of which your sense of self depends, basically.

118
00:09:41,822 --> 00:09:43,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that determine you are impermanent.

119
00:09:43,763 --> 00:09:46,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that determine you are not... are suffering.

120
00:09:46,724 --> 00:09:48,844
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you are not your own.

121
00:09:48,864 --> 00:09:52,305
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't say everything is impermanent, everything is suffering, everything is not self.

122
00:09:54,057 --> 00:09:54,177
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

123
00:09:54,237 --> 00:09:58,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Only things that you take to be yourself need to be seen as impermanent.

124
00:09:58,220 --> 00:09:59,001
[SPEAKER_00]: And what are those things?

125
00:09:59,061 --> 00:10:01,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Things rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

126
00:10:04,725 --> 00:10:08,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why when people ask me, oh, is there any, like, teacher that you think...

127
00:10:08,768 --> 00:10:15,293
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, that I would think it was right, that I would agree with, except, obviously, the Buddha or Jñānavīra.

128
00:10:15,633 --> 00:10:17,594
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say, most likely, Ajahn Chah.

129
00:10:18,235 --> 00:10:20,797
[SPEAKER_00]: But not, not for some, some, some kind of

130
00:10:21,723 --> 00:10:23,084
[SPEAKER_00]: It wouldn't occur to you to say those things.

131
00:10:53,440 --> 00:11:00,945
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's easy to misread, because his instruction, it's not... it's not very... it's not accurate.

132
00:11:01,285 --> 00:11:04,026
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... it's not like straight to the point.

133
00:11:04,066 --> 00:11:04,867
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more generic.

134
00:11:06,088 --> 00:11:09,790
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have a broader context, you can start understanding it rightly.

135
00:11:10,310 --> 00:11:13,032
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't, it's going to be much more easier to misunderstand.

136
00:11:14,899 --> 00:11:19,622
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, even the specific instructions, people will misunderstand if they have the wrong view.

137
00:11:20,002 --> 00:11:25,165
[SPEAKER_00]: But let alone instructions that are not as specific, which is the way he was teaching.

138
00:11:25,185 --> 00:11:27,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether maybe it's lost in translation, I don't know.

139
00:11:27,666 --> 00:11:32,749
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, it's much more open for misinterpretation, as we've seen first-hand for a number of years.

140
00:11:34,149 --> 00:11:39,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Like people who live with him, still, when they see what he... talk what he was teaching, you realize that... that's not... not at all.

141
00:11:42,108 --> 00:11:49,230
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can tell that despite never met Ajahn Chah, but reading that, understanding the suttas, you could see what a broader context is.

142
00:11:49,791 --> 00:11:51,431
[SPEAKER_00]: That, to most people, remains invisible.

143
00:11:55,533 --> 00:11:58,074
[SPEAKER_02]: So, here's another talk from Ajahn Chah.

144
00:11:59,774 --> 00:12:04,096
[SPEAKER_02]: And this is a... it's a series of questions and answers.

145
00:12:05,916 --> 00:12:08,017
[SPEAKER_02]: This one's called Monastery of Confusion.

146
00:12:10,657 --> 00:12:12,978
[SPEAKER_02]: So, please, just feel free to stop me.

147
00:12:13,218 --> 00:12:13,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

148
00:12:15,059 --> 00:12:17,940
[SPEAKER_02]: And comment on any points.

149
00:12:21,661 --> 00:12:28,023
[SPEAKER_02]: So, Ajahn Chah, are there any doubts about practice you need to resolve now?

150
00:12:28,043 --> 00:12:28,783
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the questioner.

151
00:12:29,804 --> 00:12:36,966
[SPEAKER_02]: When the mind isn't thinking much, that is in sort of a dark and dull state, is there something we should do to brighten it?

152
00:12:37,006 --> 00:12:38,006
[SPEAKER_02]: Or should we just sit with it?

153
00:12:39,027 --> 00:12:39,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Ajahn Chah.

154
00:12:42,095 --> 00:12:44,657
[SPEAKER_02]: Is this all the time or when you are sitting in meditation?

155
00:12:45,197 --> 00:12:46,899
[SPEAKER_02]: What exactly is this darkness like?

156
00:12:47,239 --> 00:12:50,061
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it lack of wisdom?

157
00:12:50,081 --> 00:12:50,602
[SPEAKER_02]: The questioner.

158
00:12:51,642 --> 00:12:57,147
[SPEAKER_02]: When I sit to meditate, I don't get drowsy, but my mind feels dark, sort of dense or opaque.

159
00:12:58,548 --> 00:12:59,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Ajahn Chah.

160
00:13:00,369 --> 00:13:02,791
[SPEAKER_02]: So you would like to make your mind wise, right?

161
00:13:03,895 --> 00:13:06,576
[SPEAKER_02]: Change your posture and do a lot of walking meditation.

162
00:13:07,316 --> 00:13:08,316
[SPEAKER_02]: That's one thing to do.

163
00:13:08,776 --> 00:13:11,817
[SPEAKER_02]: You can walk for three hours at a time until you're really tired.

164
00:13:13,118 --> 00:13:13,538
[SPEAKER_02]: Question.

165
00:13:15,198 --> 00:13:20,160
[SPEAKER_02]: I do walking meditation a couple of hours a day and I usually have a lot of thinking when I do it.

166
00:13:21,060 --> 00:13:24,281
[SPEAKER_02]: But what really concerns me is the dark state when I sit.

167
00:13:26,082 --> 00:13:31,463
[SPEAKER_02]: Should I just try to be aware of it and let go or is there some means I should use to counter it?

168
00:13:32,904 --> 00:13:33,424
[SPEAKER_02]: Ajahn Chah.

169
00:13:35,302 --> 00:13:38,584
[SPEAKER_02]: I think maybe your postures aren't balanced.

170
00:13:39,345 --> 00:13:40,886
[SPEAKER_02]: When you walk, you have a lot of thinking.

171
00:13:41,926 --> 00:13:44,148
[SPEAKER_02]: So you should do a lot of discursive contemplation.

172
00:13:45,408 --> 00:13:47,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Then the mind can retreat from thinking.

173
00:13:48,350 --> 00:13:49,171
[SPEAKER_02]: It won't stick there.

174
00:13:49,691 --> 00:13:50,332
[SPEAKER_02]: But never mind.

175
00:13:50,772 --> 00:13:53,634
[SPEAKER_02]: For now, increase the time you spend on the walking meditation.

176
00:13:54,434 --> 00:13:55,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Focus on that.

177
00:13:55,870 --> 00:14:02,455
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, if your mind is wandering, pull it out and do some contemplation, such as, for example, investigation of the body.

178
00:14:03,296 --> 00:14:09,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Have you ever done that continuously, rather than as an occasional reflection?

179
00:14:10,481 --> 00:14:16,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the whole point we talked about so many times.

180
00:14:16,946 --> 00:14:24,152
[SPEAKER_00]: If your view of what meditation is, it's mechanical, that's exactly going to be the result, the state of...

181
00:14:25,360 --> 00:14:30,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Drowsiness, or lack of clarity, ambiguity...

182
00:14:30,402 --> 00:14:32,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind you, for most people, they confuse that for peace.

183
00:14:32,922 --> 00:14:34,443
[SPEAKER_00]: They think that's a peaceful abiding.

184
00:14:34,843 --> 00:14:39,224
[SPEAKER_00]: But this person who is asking a question, clearly, they don't understand that it's kind of dark... he's in dark.

185
00:14:39,864 --> 00:14:42,425
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why Ajahn Chah said, oh, you mean unwise.

186
00:14:42,865 --> 00:14:45,146
[SPEAKER_00]: You want wisdom, you want clarity, you want discernment.

187
00:14:45,586 --> 00:14:46,426
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you do about it?

188
00:14:47,206 --> 00:14:52,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Did he say, watch your nostrils, or just be here now, present, holistically?

189
00:14:53,088 --> 00:14:54,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Or did he give the mantra?

190
00:14:54,209 --> 00:14:54,329
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

191
00:14:54,389 --> 00:14:55,650
[SPEAKER_00]: He said you need to think more.

192
00:14:56,390 --> 00:14:59,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Think more on the particular topics of your occasional reflection.

193
00:15:00,293 --> 00:15:10,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Spend three hours back and forth reflecting on the nature of impermanence on your body, upon which your experience as a whole sits on, depends on, and always was and always will be.

194
00:15:11,821 --> 00:15:17,845
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your past, your future, your present is equally determined by the random presence of the living body beating hard in your chest and so on.

195
00:15:20,170 --> 00:15:23,351
[SPEAKER_00]: If you think that, you will be developing the themes you think about.

196
00:15:23,851 --> 00:15:28,533
[SPEAKER_00]: If your theme is theme of dull mental states, that's the theme you will be developing about.

197
00:15:29,954 --> 00:15:41,358
[SPEAKER_00]: And those states are always within sensuality, confusion and wrong views, which means all your efforts towards meditation, if they don't go towards clarity, they will then automatically be developing, furthering the wrong views.

198
00:15:46,379 --> 00:15:47,940
[SPEAKER_00]: I think Ajahn Chah is quite polite, actually.

199
00:15:48,642 --> 00:15:51,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it was obvious how misguided those questions were.

200
00:15:52,845 --> 00:15:57,148
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was saying, well, I don't think, perhaps, maybe, your posture or something... No.

201
00:15:57,328 --> 00:15:58,669
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just clearly, it's just...

202
00:15:59,930 --> 00:16:03,032
[SPEAKER_00]: When he sits, there is zero self-awareness.

203
00:16:03,192 --> 00:16:06,575
[SPEAKER_00]: It just goes into a state, which most, most, many people do.

204
00:16:07,455 --> 00:16:15,321
[SPEAKER_00]: You see them on a meditation floor, and the... just nodding, left and right, bobbing and weaving.

205
00:16:16,613 --> 00:16:18,214
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha did describe that in the suttas.

206
00:16:18,234 --> 00:16:18,474
[SPEAKER_02]: Flawed.

207
00:16:19,334 --> 00:16:19,794
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha did...

208
00:16:19,814 --> 00:16:20,534
[SPEAKER_02]: Flawed meditation.

209
00:16:20,554 --> 00:16:21,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Flawed meditation, yeah.

210
00:16:21,415 --> 00:16:22,975
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha did describe that in the suttas.

211
00:16:23,035 --> 00:16:26,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, they call themselves meditators, but what happens?

212
00:16:26,977 --> 00:16:31,818
[SPEAKER_00]: They premeditate, mismeditate, overmeditate, undermeditate, because they end up meditating with sensuality.

213
00:16:33,199 --> 00:16:39,061
[SPEAKER_00]: They're dull, not developed faculties, and they're just sort of nodding, shoulders drooping, sleeping.

214
00:16:40,081 --> 00:16:43,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, the only way to counter that

215
00:16:44,936 --> 00:16:48,159
[SPEAKER_00]: As that person asked, it's not through some counter-method.

216
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:53,083
[SPEAKER_00]: It's through that active self-reflection dwelling on the certain themes.

217
00:16:53,584 --> 00:17:07,976
[SPEAKER_00]: If you dwell on the theme of withdrawal from sensuality, which is fundamentally rooted in the assumption in your thoughts of sensuality, you will then enter the first jhāna, accompanied by thinking and pondering.

218
00:17:08,296 --> 00:17:08,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Big surprise.

219
00:17:09,780 --> 00:17:13,444
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not by kind of blackness, darkness, dullness, head nodding.

220
00:17:13,464 --> 00:17:13,584
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

221
00:17:13,704 --> 00:17:17,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity of mindfulness and thinking and pondering that has transcendent sensuality.

222
00:17:18,029 --> 00:17:18,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

223
00:17:18,229 --> 00:17:19,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it understood the roots of it.

224
00:17:20,531 --> 00:17:21,873
[SPEAKER_00]: It understood the extent of it.

225
00:17:22,594 --> 00:17:25,897
[SPEAKER_00]: It understood the cessation of it.

226
00:17:26,057 --> 00:17:28,640
[SPEAKER_00]: You dwell on that theme, you can enter it at will.

227
00:17:29,441 --> 00:17:31,223
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to remember it, which you would.

228
00:17:35,882 --> 00:17:37,343
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll continue with these questions.

229
00:17:38,424 --> 00:17:38,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Questioner.

230
00:17:39,624 --> 00:17:42,106
[SPEAKER_02]: I feel frustrated because of my state of mind.

231
00:17:42,466 --> 00:17:44,387
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not developing samādhi or wisdom.

232
00:17:45,387 --> 00:17:52,792
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for that person, he obviously doesn't know what either samādhi or wisdom is, but he has some inclination, some idea.

233
00:17:53,852 --> 00:18:00,136
[SPEAKER_00]: But most likely, for him, samādhi equals the experience of overwhelming peace that you have nothing to do with.

234
00:18:00,156 --> 00:18:01,857
[SPEAKER_00]: You were just sitting and watching your nostrils and then...

235
00:18:02,157 --> 00:18:03,718
[SPEAKER_00]: But he's not getting that anymore either.

236
00:18:05,359 --> 00:18:11,322
[SPEAKER_02]: And Ajahn Chah answers him.

237
00:18:12,222 --> 00:18:17,545
[SPEAKER_02]: When you have this condition of mind, the suffering comes about because of not knowing.

238
00:18:18,505 --> 00:18:20,666
[SPEAKER_02]: There is doubt as to why the mind is like this.

239
00:18:21,586 --> 00:18:27,529
[SPEAKER_02]: The important principle in meditation is that whatever occurs, don't be in doubt over it.

240
00:18:27,569 --> 00:18:29,050
[SPEAKER_02]: Doubt only adds to the suffering.

241
00:18:29,774 --> 00:18:32,756
[SPEAKER_02]: If the mind is bright and awake, don't doubt that.

242
00:18:33,596 --> 00:18:34,577
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a condition of mind.

243
00:18:35,157 --> 00:18:37,678
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would... what would it... How would you say differently?

244
00:18:38,359 --> 00:18:39,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't doubt that.

245
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:41,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't doubt what's in front of you.

246
00:18:43,642 --> 00:18:44,962
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you know it clearly.

247
00:18:45,583 --> 00:18:45,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Learn...

248
00:18:47,884 --> 00:18:49,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Learn to see it on its own terms.

249
00:18:50,445 --> 00:18:51,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Allow it to arise.

250
00:18:51,526 --> 00:18:52,587
[SPEAKER_00]: All those things we talk about.

251
00:18:53,327 --> 00:18:55,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can doubt various... Oh, is it this?

252
00:18:55,708 --> 00:18:56,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it that?

253
00:18:56,029 --> 00:18:58,190
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't doubt that that thing is in front of you.

254
00:18:58,965 --> 00:19:01,427
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what many people would still be affecting with their own doubt.

255
00:19:03,769 --> 00:19:14,017
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you need to do is stop habitually giving in to the doubt, in order to see that doubt is secondary to the arisen phenomenon, the state of mind, for example.

256
00:19:14,878 --> 00:19:15,919
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't doubt that.

257
00:19:17,039 --> 00:19:23,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Sādhanācārya is pointing out that you need to learn how to think on those terms, on the terms of the things that have arisen on their own.

258
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:29,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even if you doubt, that attitude of doubt will be secondary to those things.

259
00:19:31,245 --> 00:19:33,567
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the mind is bright and clear, you recognize it.

260
00:19:33,587 --> 00:19:34,668
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is bright and clear.

261
00:19:35,189 --> 00:19:39,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you might be doubting about different things, but still, the basis is the basis that's not doubted.

262
00:19:40,573 --> 00:19:45,397
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind is affected with confusion, ambiguity, darkness, whatever it is, that is the basis.

263
00:19:46,758 --> 00:19:52,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for most people, doubt is fueled by the desire to get rid of the state that they don't want.

264
00:19:53,822 --> 00:20:00,906
[SPEAKER_00]: But the desire to get rid of the state you don't want will be there, because the state that you want hasn't been understood either.

265
00:20:00,966 --> 00:20:03,888
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no understanding developed in either of the states.

266
00:20:03,988 --> 00:20:05,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Wanted states or non-wanted states.

267
00:20:05,969 --> 00:20:12,613
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even when they had a peaceful state, they have no understanding of it, which means they don't recognize it arisen on its own terms as a phenomenon.

268
00:20:13,454 --> 00:20:23,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, although they're not actively doubting it, as in, they're not actively trying to get rid of it, they're still susceptible to doubt, because they haven't clarified it, they haven't understood it.

269
00:20:27,248 --> 00:20:28,169
[SPEAKER_02]: The doubt has arisen.

270
00:20:29,250 --> 00:20:31,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the doubt, exactly, you can recognize it as an arisen phenomenon.

271
00:20:31,952 --> 00:20:32,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can't doubt that.

272
00:20:32,593 --> 00:20:33,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't doubt that.

273
00:20:34,635 --> 00:20:40,200
[SPEAKER_00]: But that would require you to allow these things that arise, that have arisen, to endure on their own, not just...

274
00:20:40,220 --> 00:20:46,946
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's a hard thing to do, when your whole notion of practice and meditation is about doing something.

275
00:20:47,006 --> 00:20:49,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I do this, I do this, I do this, I do... No, you don't do anything.

276
00:20:49,709 --> 00:20:51,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't do anything, I don't do anything.

277
00:20:51,230 --> 00:20:52,111
[SPEAKER_00]: That becomes your doing.

278
00:20:52,955 --> 00:20:59,920
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to unwind that attitude, but not to the point of dismissing everything and like nothing is to be done, we are already in nibbāna and whatnot.

279
00:21:01,381 --> 00:21:04,623
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize it, but then you need to allow it to endure on its own.

280
00:21:06,124 --> 00:21:08,846
[SPEAKER_00]: And keep your craving to do something on account of it in check.

281
00:21:10,467 --> 00:21:12,589
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you get to discern things that's present.

282
00:21:12,889 --> 00:21:14,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Presently arisen states, as the Buddha would describe.

283
00:21:17,272 --> 00:21:18,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Ajahn Chah continues.

284
00:21:20,424 --> 00:21:25,568
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's dark and dull, your mind, don't doubt about that.

285
00:21:25,628 --> 00:21:29,952
[SPEAKER_02]: Just continue to practice diligently without getting caught up in reactions to that state.

286
00:21:30,472 --> 00:21:30,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

287
00:21:31,293 --> 00:21:35,657
[SPEAKER_02]: Taking note and being aware of your state of mind, don't have doubts about it.

288
00:21:35,697 --> 00:21:36,677
[SPEAKER_02]: It is just what it is.

289
00:21:37,258 --> 00:21:42,582
[SPEAKER_02]: When you entertain entertain doubts and start grasping at it and giving it meaning, then it's dark.

290
00:21:43,563 --> 00:21:43,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

291
00:21:44,844 --> 00:21:45,925
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly what you just said.

292
00:21:46,465 --> 00:21:49,468
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if it's... no matter what the state is, bright or dark,

293
00:21:50,297 --> 00:21:55,900
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize it on its own, allow it to endure, don't give in to reaction, as he said, towards it.

294
00:21:55,940 --> 00:21:59,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how the darkness and doubt really overwhelms you.

295
00:22:00,602 --> 00:22:05,164
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't mean either just ignore everything and just be at ease with everything.

296
00:22:05,924 --> 00:22:17,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in order to allow things to endure on their own, in order to not keep maintaining your reactions towards them, you need to develop discernment of their nature.

297
00:22:18,238 --> 00:22:33,049
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will not develop that discernment if you go completely the other way and mystify the whole thing, whereby just being here and now in pristine awareness and whatnot, holistically present and in just the present moment.

298
00:22:34,010 --> 00:22:36,832
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's completely the other way now.

299
00:22:36,872 --> 00:22:39,174
[SPEAKER_00]: First is you try to get rid of it, of everything.

300
00:22:39,735 --> 00:22:41,476
[SPEAKER_00]: This one is you try to accept everything.

301
00:22:41,816 --> 00:22:46,380
[SPEAKER_00]: But you should not be in a holistically present moment of your awareness in regard to sensuality.

302
00:22:47,294 --> 00:22:50,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you are, means you're welcoming it, you're entertaining it, you're bound by it.

303
00:22:54,700 --> 00:23:00,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to practice this sermon, but not overdo it, nor underdo it, hence the middle way.

304
00:23:02,145 --> 00:23:16,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And you recognize that on the states of the mind, whether it's doubt, whether it's, like, contentment, whether it's sensuality, whether it's ill-will, whatever state it is, you recognize it and so, which means there is, there has to be some effort of this sermon and contemplation,

305
00:23:17,692 --> 00:23:22,836
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that effort starts to sort of pick itself up and starts doubting itself, that's where you stop.

306
00:23:23,817 --> 00:23:27,960
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't develop a view that you should always stop, or you should always push forward.

307
00:23:29,661 --> 00:23:31,883
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why when they ask the Buddha, so how did you cross the shore?

308
00:23:32,303 --> 00:23:38,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by not pushing forward, by not pulling backwards, by not standing still.

309
00:23:40,529 --> 00:23:45,633
[SPEAKER_02]: So this is from the same talk, Monastery of Confusion, and the questioner continues.

310
00:23:47,767 --> 00:23:58,876
[SPEAKER_02]: Ajahn Chah, you're sitting and suddenly the thought of someone pops into your head.

311
00:23:59,316 --> 00:24:01,178
[SPEAKER_02]: That's vitakka, the initial thought.

312
00:24:02,198 --> 00:24:05,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you take the idea of that the person and start thinking about them in detail.

313
00:24:06,502 --> 00:24:08,743
[SPEAKER_02]: Vitakka is picking it up, picking it up.

314
00:24:09,204 --> 00:24:11,045
[SPEAKER_02]: Vitakka is investigating it.

315
00:24:12,406 --> 00:24:15,689
[SPEAKER_02]: For example, we pick up the idea of death and then we start considering it.

316
00:24:16,518 --> 00:24:17,038
[SPEAKER_02]: I will die.

317
00:24:17,458 --> 00:24:18,039
[SPEAKER_02]: Others will die.

318
00:24:18,719 --> 00:24:19,959
[SPEAKER_02]: Every living being will die.

319
00:24:20,880 --> 00:24:22,721
[SPEAKER_02]: When they die, where will they go?

320
00:24:22,741 --> 00:24:23,261
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, stop.

321
00:24:24,581 --> 00:24:25,702
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop and bring it back again.

322
00:24:26,662 --> 00:24:28,643
[SPEAKER_02]: When it gets running like that, stop it again.

323
00:24:29,423 --> 00:24:31,224
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, go back to mindfulness of the breath.

324
00:24:32,144 --> 00:24:35,166
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, the discursive thought will wander off and not come back.

325
00:24:35,866 --> 00:24:36,666
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to stop it.

326
00:24:37,526 --> 00:24:39,587
[SPEAKER_02]: Keep at it until the mind is bright and clear.

327
00:24:41,568 --> 00:24:41,788
[SPEAKER_02]: If you...

328
00:24:41,808 --> 00:24:45,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So, keep... keep the discursive thinking until the mind is bright and clear.

329
00:24:48,058 --> 00:24:48,198
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

330
00:24:48,938 --> 00:25:02,184
[SPEAKER_02]: If you practice vichara with an object that you are suited to, you may experience the hairs of your body standing on end, tears pouring from your eyes, a state of extreme delight, many different things as joy comes about.

331
00:25:02,484 --> 00:25:03,345
[SPEAKER_02]: From proper thinking.

332
00:25:03,365 --> 00:25:04,145
[SPEAKER_02]: From proper thinking.

333
00:25:06,206 --> 00:25:10,288
[SPEAKER_00]: I know that at least, I heard at least number of people, but I could see that being quite a common attitude.

334
00:25:10,308 --> 00:25:12,428
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would even read that, what he said quite clearly.

335
00:25:13,189 --> 00:25:14,509
[SPEAKER_00]: You learn how to think properly.

336
00:25:15,450 --> 00:25:15,930
[SPEAKER_00]: You pick up

337
00:25:18,328 --> 00:25:23,292
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to say object, because it's already... that notion is already infused with the wrong view for many people.

338
00:25:23,912 --> 00:25:28,977
[SPEAKER_00]: But you pick up a theme, and then you think about it, you clarify, you ponder on it, thinking and pondering.

339
00:25:29,717 --> 00:25:32,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not denying it, you're not doing it for the sake of trying to get rid of it.

340
00:25:34,221 --> 00:25:40,486
[SPEAKER_00]: On the contrary, you want to develop the clarity in regard to that which is present, in regard to that which is enduring, which has arisen.

341
00:25:41,447 --> 00:25:44,189
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't develop clarity if your attitude is of trying to get rid of it.

342
00:25:44,889 --> 00:25:46,830
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people will be reading this as clear as it is.

343
00:25:46,890 --> 00:25:48,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So, proper vittaka vichara.

344
00:25:49,312 --> 00:25:55,355
[SPEAKER_00]: And still, practice their meditation for the purpose of shutting all down, all thoughts.

345
00:25:55,395 --> 00:25:56,876
[SPEAKER_00]: I must stop thinking, stop thinking.

346
00:25:57,977 --> 00:26:01,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that that's another thought, that they are thinking at the time.

347
00:26:03,180 --> 00:26:08,283
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't stop your thinking because even the first jhāna is not about stopping the thinking.

348
00:26:08,323 --> 00:26:09,384
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about thinking and pondering.

349
00:26:11,565 --> 00:26:14,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Away from the basis of lust, aversion and delusion.

350
00:26:16,929 --> 00:26:19,732
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, within that, you'll know how to stop that thinking.

351
00:26:19,772 --> 00:26:21,373
[SPEAKER_00]: But even that, it's not you stopping it.

352
00:26:22,534 --> 00:26:31,261
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just you discerning mindfulness and imperturbable... theme of imperturbable even... even... even higher than the domain of thinking and pondering.

353
00:26:32,542 --> 00:26:36,685
[SPEAKER_00]: But you won't get the idea of what that is until you learn how to think and ponder properly.

354
00:26:39,166 --> 00:26:49,989
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, like, he gave the simile for the jhāna, like, eh... when a mountain cow, before it sets its foot on one stone, thinks about stepping on the second one and then falls in between.

355
00:26:51,549 --> 00:27:01,732
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's when people, like, already have notions of what cessation of thinking and pondering is, while they even haven't understood or learned how to do rightly thinking and pondering in the first place.

356
00:27:09,943 --> 00:27:10,923
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all we talk about.

357
00:27:11,403 --> 00:27:12,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Learning how to think.

358
00:27:12,523 --> 00:27:14,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Take a theme, dwell on it.

359
00:27:15,144 --> 00:27:16,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-questioning, that we always do.

360
00:27:17,844 --> 00:27:18,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask, and then stop.

361
00:27:18,845 --> 00:27:21,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't rush in giving the answer, or this is this is this is this.

362
00:27:22,065 --> 00:27:24,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's almost on the level of psychologizing.

363
00:27:24,706 --> 00:27:31,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Just ask these questions, because they will force you to endure the presently arisen situation that you are questioning.

364
00:27:31,147 --> 00:27:38,728
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you provide the answer, that often means, oh, you jumped on it, and now you're going to go for the other answer, and you lost the context, you lost the sight of the peripheral.

365
00:27:40,450 --> 00:27:46,675
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you stay with that questioning, you don't... you're not doing it for the sake of self-mystification.

366
00:27:46,715 --> 00:27:49,196
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to know, but you don't want to jump at the answer.

367
00:27:49,677 --> 00:27:51,278
[SPEAKER_00]: You allow the question to endure.

368
00:27:52,659 --> 00:27:58,063
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how questions get answered on their own terms, not on your preconceived terms.

369
00:27:59,584 --> 00:28:00,825
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's Vītāka-vicara properly.

370
00:28:05,633 --> 00:28:06,853
[SPEAKER_02]: The questions continue.

371
00:28:07,514 --> 00:28:12,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Questioner, can this happen with any kind of thinking, the rapture, the joy?

372
00:28:13,655 --> 00:28:18,617
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is it only in a state of tranquility that it happens?

373
00:28:18,637 --> 00:28:21,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Ajahn Chah, it's when the mind is tranquil.

374
00:28:22,538 --> 00:28:24,978
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not ordinary mental proliferation.

375
00:28:25,158 --> 00:28:25,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

376
00:28:26,339 --> 00:28:29,499
[SPEAKER_02]: You sit with a calm mind and then the initial thought comes.

377
00:28:29,900 --> 00:28:31,520
[SPEAKER_02]: For example, I think...

378
00:28:31,540 --> 00:28:35,061
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there will be, sorry, there will be another thing that the person needs to start discerning first.

379
00:28:35,817 --> 00:28:36,998
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a proliferation?

380
00:28:37,638 --> 00:28:39,299
[SPEAKER_00]: What is thinking and pondering mindful?

381
00:28:40,259 --> 00:28:44,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindful thinking and pondering, there is no base for proliferation.

382
00:28:44,981 --> 00:28:50,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence of that peripheral awareness in regard to thinking and pondering, that's the proliferation.

383
00:28:51,644 --> 00:28:54,385
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not... proliferation means thinking.

384
00:28:54,746 --> 00:28:57,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, no proliferation means no thinking.

385
00:28:58,247 --> 00:28:59,608
[SPEAKER_00]: You could only wish it's that easy.

386
00:29:03,461 --> 00:29:09,645
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he says, for example, I think of my brother who just passed away, or I might think of some other relatives.

387
00:29:10,425 --> 00:29:11,866
[SPEAKER_02]: This is when the mind is tranquil.

388
00:29:12,206 --> 00:29:16,369
[SPEAKER_02]: The tranquility isn't something certain, but for the moment, the mind is tranquil.

389
00:29:17,389 --> 00:29:20,691
[SPEAKER_02]: After this initial thought comes, then I go into discursive thought.

390
00:29:22,452 --> 00:29:27,535
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's a line of thinking that's skillful and wholesome, it leads to ease of mind and happiness.

391
00:29:28,135 --> 00:29:31,677
[SPEAKER_02]: And there is rapture with its attendant experiences.

392
00:29:32,779 --> 00:29:38,702
[SPEAKER_02]: This rapture came from the initial and discursive thinking that took place in a state of calmness.

393
00:29:39,662 --> 00:29:43,584
[SPEAKER_02]: We don't have to give it names such as first jhāna or second jhāna and so forth.

394
00:29:44,104 --> 00:29:45,404
[SPEAKER_02]: We just call it tranquility.

395
00:29:46,325 --> 00:29:46,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Imperturbable.

396
00:29:47,385 --> 00:29:47,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Unshakable.

397
00:29:48,466 --> 00:29:48,966
[SPEAKER_00]: At the time.

398
00:29:51,547 --> 00:29:54,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's nothing mystical about it.

399
00:29:54,288 --> 00:29:55,049
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

400
00:29:56,569 --> 00:29:58,270
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's nothing mundane about it either.

401
00:30:05,047 --> 00:30:26,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And even thoughts such as his dead brother will not necessarily disturb that tranquility unless he chose to give in to it and lost the context, lost the peripheral awareness of his own thinking, lost the sight of it, lost the theme of it.

402
00:30:27,123 --> 00:30:28,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Call it whichever way you want.

403
00:30:28,704 --> 00:30:30,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yeah, then he's affected by it.

404
00:30:38,973 --> 00:30:42,195
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is from another talk called Still Flowing Water.

405
00:30:47,717 --> 00:30:48,218
[SPEAKER_02]: Ajahn Chah.

406
00:30:49,138 --> 00:30:51,699
[SPEAKER_02]: I've often taught that tranquility is stillness.

407
00:30:52,660 --> 00:30:53,780
[SPEAKER_02]: Flowing is wisdom.

408
00:30:54,681 --> 00:30:57,442
[SPEAKER_02]: We practice meditation to calm the mind and make it still.

409
00:30:57,963 --> 00:30:58,683
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it can flow.

410
00:30:59,944 --> 00:31:03,785
[SPEAKER_02]: In the beginning, we learn what still water is like and what flowing water is like.

411
00:31:04,826 --> 00:31:08,308
[SPEAKER_02]: After practicing for a while, we see how these two support each other.

412
00:31:09,112 --> 00:31:11,573
[SPEAKER_02]: We have to make the mind calm, like still water.

413
00:31:12,174 --> 00:31:13,715
[SPEAKER_02]: Then it flows.

414
00:31:13,755 --> 00:31:17,077
[SPEAKER_02]: Both being still and flowing, this is not easy to contemplate.

415
00:31:19,638 --> 00:31:21,739
[SPEAKER_02]: We can understand that still water doesn't flow.

416
00:31:22,420 --> 00:31:24,621
[SPEAKER_02]: We can understand that flowing water isn't still.

417
00:31:25,522 --> 00:31:28,023
[SPEAKER_02]: But when we practice, we take hold of both of these.

418
00:31:29,144 --> 00:31:32,946
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind of a true practitioner is like still water that flows.

419
00:31:33,866 --> 00:31:36,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So, two things simultaneously present.

420
00:31:37,248 --> 00:31:38,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Stillness and flowing.

421
00:31:39,814 --> 00:31:41,915
[SPEAKER_00]: If you chase one of them, you're not still.

422
00:31:44,457 --> 00:31:49,139
[SPEAKER_00]: But when there is stillness, or rather when there is flowing, there is stillness.

423
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:51,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Two separate things.

424
00:31:52,381 --> 00:31:55,163
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the true practitioner is the one who discerns that.

425
00:31:56,864 --> 00:31:59,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerns stillness while flowing.

426
00:32:00,566 --> 00:32:02,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Allows flowing while still.

427
00:32:03,889 --> 00:32:05,210
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not on the same footing.

428
00:32:05,470 --> 00:32:08,591
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a contradiction in terms of A is B and B is A.

429
00:32:09,431 --> 00:32:13,453
[SPEAKER_00]: It's A and more A or less A and A.

430
00:32:17,614 --> 00:32:21,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, stillness and flowing, it's nothing other than saying what the Buddha said.

431
00:32:22,156 --> 00:32:27,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising is manifest or ceasing is manifest and changing while standing is manifest.

432
00:32:29,759 --> 00:32:32,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Persisting while staying the same is manifest.

433
00:32:33,150 --> 00:32:36,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Flowing while being still is manifest.

434
00:32:36,292 --> 00:32:37,033
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not mystical.

435
00:32:37,053 --> 00:32:48,060
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the third characteristic of, as in, the most fundamental characteristic of anything that has a nature of arising, which is literally everything in your experience.

436
00:32:49,260 --> 00:32:51,382
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not chasing the content of what arises.

437
00:32:52,142 --> 00:32:54,324
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize that basis of it.

438
00:32:55,064 --> 00:32:59,287
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether things are arising or they're ceasing,

439
00:33:00,216 --> 00:33:03,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, there is persisting while remaining the same.

440
00:33:04,977 --> 00:33:06,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Increasing, decreasing.

441
00:33:07,218 --> 00:33:14,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, the same thing is persisting while changing.

442
00:33:14,600 --> 00:33:17,740
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the definition of, like, say... We spoke about it before.

443
00:33:18,561 --> 00:33:26,043
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to get too technical about it, because then, you know... Well, people will lose the sight of this context in particular, when it comes to the context of meditation.

444
00:33:26,563 --> 00:33:27,683
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the same thing.

445
00:33:30,730 --> 00:33:37,232
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in terms of like a linear flow or something.

446
00:33:37,272 --> 00:33:38,812
[SPEAKER_00]: That means you're already in that flow.

447
00:33:39,913 --> 00:33:51,136
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could see 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 as things flowing, as in coming and going, while the context, while the peripheral context of those things remains unchanged.

448
00:33:52,356 --> 00:33:55,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the most fundamental context of your experience as a whole?

449
00:33:55,297 --> 00:33:56,077
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your current mood.

450
00:33:57,173 --> 00:34:06,981
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, sorry, as Ajahn Chah said there, like, say, currently you're tranquil, and you know that's not necessarily always going to be the case, but that's the case at the time.

451
00:34:07,562 --> 00:34:10,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Within that tranquility, you have thoughts coming and going.

452
00:34:11,966 --> 00:34:13,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So, within this, you have this.

453
00:34:18,855 --> 00:34:28,861
[SPEAKER_00]: So, within the mood that's enduring, that is... that is not changing as fast, so to speak, that it's there on its own, you have these things coming and going at a quicker rate.

454
00:34:29,681 --> 00:34:37,446
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not losing the sight of that, so you're not chasing these things coming and going, nor are you mystifying your mood and ignoring things coming and going.

455
00:34:37,586 --> 00:34:39,006
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, with this, this is.

456
00:34:40,107 --> 00:34:43,309
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, with the best of this quality, I have such and such thoughts.

457
00:34:44,389 --> 00:34:45,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's still enduring.

458
00:34:47,326 --> 00:34:51,247
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're not sure, you ask yourself, oh, is my mood still... am I still tranquil?

459
00:34:51,767 --> 00:34:54,228
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is yes, it's still the same thing enduring.

460
00:34:54,828 --> 00:34:56,508
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same stillness.

461
00:34:58,029 --> 00:35:01,890
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you start discerning stillness rightly, it will not be affected by the flow.

462
00:35:06,231 --> 00:35:09,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Such is the example of him being tranquil, thinking about his dead brother.

463
00:35:10,672 --> 00:35:15,753
[SPEAKER_00]: If he was not established in tranquility, even if it's not tranquility, if it's any other mood, if he were established properly,

464
00:35:17,201 --> 00:35:24,165
[SPEAKER_00]: It would have been the principle of that development of that imperturbable, as in something steadier than these particular things.

465
00:35:25,166 --> 00:35:38,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in his stillness, even the thoughts of... the flowing thoughts of his dead brother and memories did not overwhelm the mind, did not uproot that establishment of two things simultaneously present together.

466
00:35:40,175 --> 00:35:42,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Greater context and things within the context.

467
00:35:43,480 --> 00:35:44,901
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep using the word context.

468
00:35:45,361 --> 00:35:47,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's no context without a content of the context.

469
00:35:48,222 --> 00:35:50,163
[SPEAKER_00]: But the content of the context is not the context.

470
00:35:52,204 --> 00:35:52,804
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly.

471
00:35:52,824 --> 00:35:56,626
[SPEAKER_00]: The context is still in regard to the flowing content.

472
00:35:57,426 --> 00:35:59,807
[SPEAKER_00]: And it gives the meaning to the flowing content.

473
00:36:00,148 --> 00:36:00,828
[SPEAKER_00]: That stillness.

474
00:36:02,667 --> 00:36:12,335
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to discern that rightly, you need to see... you need to see the domain of flow and domain of stillness as two simultaneous things present together, but not the same, not completely apart either.

475
00:36:12,636 --> 00:36:14,657
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what we meant... that meant for a true practitioner.

476
00:36:14,677 --> 00:36:16,599
[SPEAKER_00]: That means... that's meant for somebody with the right view.

477
00:36:20,802 --> 00:36:21,323
[SPEAKER_02]: He continues.

478
00:36:22,364 --> 00:36:25,607
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind of a true practitioner is like still water that flows.

479
00:36:25,927 --> 00:36:26,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

480
00:36:26,667 --> 00:36:30,651
[SPEAKER_00]: A flowing water that's still... You cannot lose the sight of the context, nor mind of the flow.

481
00:36:32,670 --> 00:36:37,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever takes place in in the mind of a dhamma practitioner is like flowing water that is still.

482
00:36:38,634 --> 00:36:40,695
[SPEAKER_02]: To say that it is only flowing is not correct.

483
00:36:40,735 --> 00:36:41,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

484
00:36:41,235 --> 00:36:42,396
[SPEAKER_02]: Only still is not correct.

485
00:36:42,416 --> 00:36:42,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

486
00:36:42,696 --> 00:36:49,400
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I said context is... you can't conceive context without the content of it, but content of it has no meaning without a context.

487
00:36:50,401 --> 00:36:52,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying there is stillness without a flow, it's meaningless.

488
00:36:53,442 --> 00:36:55,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Or there is just flow without stillness, it's equally meaningless.

489
00:36:56,825 --> 00:36:57,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Equally wrong.

490
00:37:02,725 --> 00:37:08,087
[SPEAKER_02]: But when we have experience of practice, our minds will be in this condition of flowing water that is still.

491
00:37:10,288 --> 00:37:11,569
[SPEAKER_02]: This is something we've never seen.

492
00:37:12,049 --> 00:37:14,210
[SPEAKER_02]: When we see flowing water, it is just flowing along.

493
00:37:14,390 --> 00:37:16,330
[SPEAKER_02]: When we see still water, it doesn't flow.

494
00:37:16,871 --> 00:37:20,912
[SPEAKER_02]: But within our minds, it will really be like this, like flowing water that is still.

495
00:37:22,093 --> 00:37:27,155
[SPEAKER_02]: In our dhamma practice, we have samādhi or tranquility and wisdom mixed together.

496
00:37:27,555 --> 00:37:29,776
[SPEAKER_02]: We have morality, meditation and wisdom.

497
00:37:30,788 --> 00:37:33,449
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, whenever we sit, the mind is still and it flows.

498
00:37:34,469 --> 00:37:35,389
[SPEAKER_02]: Still flowing water.

499
00:37:36,309 --> 00:37:41,750
[SPEAKER_02]: With meditative stability and wisdom, tranquility and insight, it's like this.

500
00:37:42,470 --> 00:37:43,431
[SPEAKER_02]: The dhamma is like this.

501
00:37:44,231 --> 00:37:47,511
[SPEAKER_02]: If you have reached the dhamma, then at all times you will have this experience.

502
00:37:48,252 --> 00:37:50,012
[SPEAKER_02]: Being tranquil and having wisdom.

503
00:37:50,532 --> 00:37:51,692
[SPEAKER_02]: Flowing, yet still.

504
00:37:52,372 --> 00:37:53,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Not suffering amidst suffering.

505
00:37:58,540 --> 00:38:00,562
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the purpose of tranquility?

506
00:38:01,923 --> 00:38:02,983
[SPEAKER_02]: Why should we have wisdom?

507
00:38:04,084 --> 00:38:07,407
[SPEAKER_02]: They are only for the purpose of freeing ourselves from suffering, nothing else.

508
00:38:08,628 --> 00:38:14,752
[SPEAKER_02]: At present, we are suffering, living with dukkha, not understanding dukkha, and therefore holding on to it.

509
00:38:15,611 --> 00:38:20,494
[SPEAKER_02]: But if the mind is as I've been speaking about, then there will be many kinds of knowledge.

510
00:38:21,234 --> 00:38:28,719
[SPEAKER_02]: One will know suffering, know the cause of suffering, know the cessation of suffering, know the way of practice to reach the end of that suffering.

511
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:30,840
[SPEAKER_02]: These are the noble truths.

512
00:38:31,641 --> 00:38:35,003
[SPEAKER_02]: They will appear of themselves when there is still flowing water.

513
00:38:36,364 --> 00:38:43,248
[SPEAKER_00]: When the principle of simultaneous dependence has been understood, one who sees paticca upāda sees the dhamma, as the Buddha said himself.

514
00:38:46,951 --> 00:38:47,811
[SPEAKER_00]: With this, this is.

515
00:38:49,192 --> 00:38:50,292
[SPEAKER_00]: With stillness, flowing is.

516
00:38:51,313 --> 00:38:54,454
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is flow, there is stillness in regard to it.

517
00:38:54,474 --> 00:38:55,795
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just which way you're going to go.

518
00:38:56,415 --> 00:39:02,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you'll be taken with the grain of that flow, or you will be discerning against the grain of the flow, against the flow.

519
00:39:04,438 --> 00:39:08,400
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this again is from the talk, Still Flowing Water, Ajahn Chah.

520
00:39:10,741 --> 00:39:13,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people think that meditation means to sit in some special way.

521
00:39:15,885 --> 00:39:22,328
[SPEAKER_02]: But in actual fact, standing, sitting, walking and reclining are all vehicles for meditation practice.

522
00:39:23,748 --> 00:39:24,989
[SPEAKER_02]: You can practice at all times.

523
00:39:25,329 --> 00:39:28,531
[SPEAKER_02]: Samādhi literally means the firmly established mind.

524
00:39:29,591 --> 00:39:33,753
[SPEAKER_02]: To develop samādhi, you don't have to go bottling up the mind.

525
00:39:34,253 --> 00:39:34,593
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people...

526
00:39:34,633 --> 00:39:36,994
[SPEAKER_00]: It means establishment of the mind upon the context.

527
00:39:38,975 --> 00:39:45,298
[SPEAKER_00]: There is... it is imperturbable in regard to sitting, walking, laying down, standing.

528
00:39:47,141 --> 00:39:49,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the context of the four postures?

529
00:39:50,222 --> 00:39:53,303
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the presently enduring body, as a whole.

530
00:39:54,263 --> 00:39:56,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in a specific posture.

531
00:39:56,124 --> 00:39:58,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Specific posture is secondary to the present body.

532
00:39:59,365 --> 00:40:10,529
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're mindful of the body properly, if you're discerning the theme of the present body, on account of reflection, body parts, whatever you take on, you will be then mindful of all the postures within it.

533
00:40:14,175 --> 00:40:19,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Means the mind is established peripherally in regard to what's presently actually manifesting in front of you.

534
00:40:19,239 --> 00:40:24,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Standing, walking, talking, sitting, extending your arm, eating, all these things.

535
00:40:24,423 --> 00:40:25,745
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't lose your mindfulness.

536
00:40:25,765 --> 00:40:26,305
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

537
00:40:26,325 --> 00:40:28,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your mindfulness is not in the content.

538
00:40:28,707 --> 00:40:28,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

539
00:40:30,288 --> 00:40:32,370
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in regard to the context of the content.

540
00:40:33,171 --> 00:40:34,492
[SPEAKER_00]: The meaning behind it.

541
00:40:35,933 --> 00:40:41,578
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't make the effort to discern that theme, that context, you are going with the flow.

542
00:40:42,378 --> 00:40:43,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when you're doing the

543
00:40:44,872 --> 00:40:46,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Meditation practice is the best of your efforts.

544
00:40:49,696 --> 00:40:50,276
[SPEAKER_02]: He continues.

545
00:40:53,378 --> 00:40:58,682
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people try to get peaceful by sitting quietly and having nothing disturb them at all.

546
00:40:59,643 --> 00:41:00,964
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's just like being dead.

547
00:41:01,685 --> 00:41:04,767
[SPEAKER_02]: The practice of samādhi is for developing wisdom and understanding.

548
00:41:07,389 --> 00:41:10,051
[SPEAKER_02]: Samādhi is the firm mind, the one-pointed mind.

549
00:41:10,952 --> 00:41:12,293
[SPEAKER_02]: On which point is it fixed?

550
00:41:13,092 --> 00:41:14,594
[SPEAKER_02]: It's fixed on the point of balance.

551
00:41:15,314 --> 00:41:16,015
[SPEAKER_02]: That's its point.

552
00:41:17,076 --> 00:41:20,079
[SPEAKER_02]: But people practice meditation to try silence their minds.

553
00:41:20,539 --> 00:41:24,463
[SPEAKER_02]: They say, I try to sit in meditation, but my mind won't be still for a minute.

554
00:41:25,524 --> 00:41:27,766
[SPEAKER_02]: One instant, it flies off one place.

555
00:41:28,006 --> 00:41:30,068
[SPEAKER_02]: The next instant, it flies off somewhere else.

556
00:41:31,330 --> 00:41:32,611
[SPEAKER_02]: How can I make it stop still?

557
00:41:33,672 --> 00:41:34,793
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to make it stop.

558
00:41:35,153 --> 00:41:35,874
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not the point.

559
00:41:35,894 --> 00:41:37,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, why would you?

560
00:41:39,545 --> 00:41:40,625
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is never still.

561
00:41:40,805 --> 00:41:44,566
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind thinks, the eyes see, the nose smells, and so on.

562
00:41:45,407 --> 00:41:47,767
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's you being affected by the moving mind.

563
00:41:47,987 --> 00:41:48,628
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem.

564
00:41:49,248 --> 00:41:50,688
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't see that that's the problem.

565
00:41:50,708 --> 00:41:53,469
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see that it's your attitude in regard to your own mind the problem.

566
00:41:54,209 --> 00:41:55,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you blame the mind for it.

567
00:41:55,710 --> 00:42:06,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you try to stop your thinking, you try to control your environment where nothing will disturb you, because you will not be able to remain still in regard to the flowing of your thought.

568
00:42:08,537 --> 00:42:09,878
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you try to deny the thought.

569
00:42:10,699 --> 00:42:11,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to suppress it.

570
00:42:11,579 --> 00:42:12,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to silence it.

571
00:42:14,882 --> 00:42:17,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And even worse, develop views that that's what jhāna is and what not.

572
00:42:21,827 --> 00:42:24,269
[SPEAKER_02]: It continues, eh... You don't have to make it stop.

573
00:42:24,409 --> 00:42:25,170
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not the point.

574
00:42:26,471 --> 00:42:30,133
[SPEAKER_02]: Where there is movement, movement is where understanding can arise.

575
00:42:31,114 --> 00:42:31,855
[SPEAKER_02]: People complain.

576
00:42:32,941 --> 00:42:34,562
[SPEAKER_02]: It runs off and I pull it back again.

577
00:42:34,883 --> 00:42:36,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it goes off again and I pull it back once more.

578
00:42:37,144 --> 00:42:39,506
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they just sit there, pulling back and forth like this.

579
00:42:44,910 --> 00:42:47,092
[SPEAKER_02]: You must contemplate in order to find peace.

580
00:42:48,273 --> 00:42:54,438
[SPEAKER_02]: What people usually refer to as peace is simply the calming of the mind, not the calming of the defilements.

581
00:42:55,739 --> 00:43:02,244
[SPEAKER_02]: The defilements are simply being temporarily subdued, like... Yeah, through ignoring them, through denying them.

582
00:43:03,684 --> 00:43:05,905
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I wouldn't call it even a temporary peace.

583
00:43:07,345 --> 00:43:09,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I suppose you could call it, but it's not the peace-peace.

584
00:43:10,286 --> 00:43:10,566
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

585
00:43:11,807 --> 00:43:20,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by denying thoughts, by trying to not think, by shutting everything out, yeah, you will not even see what the problem is.

586
00:43:20,950 --> 00:43:24,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what it amounts to is ignoring the problem means there is no problem.

587
00:43:25,131 --> 00:43:25,531
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

588
00:43:27,972 --> 00:43:30,193
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, just like grass covered by a rock,

589
00:43:31,095 --> 00:43:32,936
[SPEAKER_02]: In three or four days, you take the rock off.

590
00:43:33,356 --> 00:43:37,558
[SPEAKER_02]: The grass, in no long time, it grows, grows up again.

591
00:43:38,138 --> 00:43:39,439
[SPEAKER_02]: The grass hasn't really died.

592
00:43:40,319 --> 00:43:41,660
[SPEAKER_02]: It was simply being suppressed.

593
00:43:43,060 --> 00:43:44,641
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same when sitting in meditation.

594
00:43:45,481 --> 00:43:48,302
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind is calmed, but the defilements are not really calmed.

595
00:43:49,043 --> 00:43:51,024
[SPEAKER_02]: Therefore, samādhi is not a sure thing.

596
00:43:51,824 --> 00:43:53,985
[SPEAKER_02]: To find real peace, you must develop wisdom.

597
00:43:55,592 --> 00:43:58,634
[SPEAKER_02]: Samādhi is one kind of peace, like the rock covering the grass.

598
00:43:59,115 --> 00:44:02,477
[SPEAKER_02]: In a few days, you take the rock away, and the grass grows up again.

599
00:44:03,738 --> 00:44:05,359
[SPEAKER_02]: This is only a temporary peace.

600
00:44:06,180 --> 00:44:12,625
[SPEAKER_02]: The peace of wisdom is like putting the rock down and not lifting it up, just leaving it where it is.

601
00:44:12,665 --> 00:44:15,327
[SPEAKER_02]: The grass cannot possibly grow again.

602
00:44:15,987 --> 00:44:20,871
[SPEAKER_02]: This is real peace, the calming of defilements, the sure peace which results from wisdom.

603
00:44:25,562 --> 00:44:35,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And wisdom is not a metaphysical experience or some sort of transcendental release at the end of your meditation technique.

604
00:44:36,246 --> 00:44:39,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Wisdom is effort to understand broader fulfillment.

605
00:44:40,568 --> 00:44:41,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding.

606
00:44:43,629 --> 00:44:45,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding the nature of the arisen thing.

607
00:44:45,290 --> 00:44:46,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding where the problem is.

608
00:44:47,371 --> 00:44:48,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding the origin of the problem.

609
00:44:49,092 --> 00:44:50,212
[SPEAKER_00]: The cessation of the problem.

610
00:44:51,112 --> 00:44:52,153
[SPEAKER_00]: And the way out of the problem.

611
00:44:58,062 --> 00:44:58,903
[SPEAKER_02]: The talk continues.

612
00:45:00,263 --> 00:45:06,725
[SPEAKER_02]: In any case, in our practice, no matter what aspect you refer to, you must always begin from the mind.

613
00:45:07,905 --> 00:45:09,206
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you know what this mind is?

614
00:45:10,046 --> 00:45:10,966
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the mind like?

615
00:45:11,466 --> 00:45:11,827
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it?

616
00:45:12,127 --> 00:45:12,587
[SPEAKER_02]: Where is it?

617
00:45:13,087 --> 00:45:14,147
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody knows.

618
00:45:15,328 --> 00:45:18,029
[SPEAKER_02]: All we know is that we want to go over here and over there.

619
00:45:18,269 --> 00:45:19,629
[SPEAKER_02]: We want this and we want that.

620
00:45:19,869 --> 00:45:21,109
[SPEAKER_02]: We feel good or we feel bad.

621
00:45:21,850 --> 00:45:24,010
[SPEAKER_02]: But the mind itself seems impossible to know.

622
00:45:24,951 --> 00:45:25,631
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the mind?

623
00:45:26,626 --> 00:45:27,827
[SPEAKER_02]: The mind doesn't have form.

624
00:45:28,427 --> 00:45:31,750
[SPEAKER_02]: That which receives impressions, both good and bad, we call mind.

625
00:45:32,790 --> 00:45:34,011
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like the owner of a house.

626
00:45:34,872 --> 00:45:38,614
[SPEAKER_01]: The owner stays put at home while visitors come to see him.

627
00:45:39,615 --> 00:45:43,598
[SPEAKER_02]: He's the one who receives the visitors, who receives sense impressions.

628
00:45:44,779 --> 00:45:47,761
[SPEAKER_02]: But what is it that perceives?

629
00:45:47,781 --> 00:45:49,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Who lets go of sense impressions?

630
00:45:50,963 --> 00:45:52,364
[SPEAKER_02]: That is what we call mind.

631
00:45:53,376 --> 00:45:54,316
[SPEAKER_02]: But people can't see it.

632
00:45:54,416 --> 00:45:56,157
[SPEAKER_02]: They think themselves around in circles.

633
00:45:56,757 --> 00:45:57,437
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the mind?

634
00:45:57,537 --> 00:45:58,198
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the brain?

635
00:45:59,138 --> 00:46:00,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't confuse the issue like this.

636
00:46:01,619 --> 00:46:03,840
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it that receives impressions?

637
00:46:04,740 --> 00:46:07,841
[SPEAKER_02]: Some impressions it likes and some it doesn't like.

638
00:46:08,441 --> 00:46:08,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Who is that?

639
00:46:09,982 --> 00:46:12,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there one who likes and dislikes?

640
00:46:12,403 --> 00:46:12,903
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure there is.

641
00:46:13,423 --> 00:46:14,163
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can't see it.

642
00:46:14,784 --> 00:46:15,704
[SPEAKER_02]: That is what we call mind.

643
00:46:20,382 --> 00:46:24,643
[SPEAKER_02]: Therefore, it is said to begin the practice by calming the mind.

644
00:46:25,703 --> 00:46:27,244
[SPEAKER_02]: Put awareness into the mind.

645
00:46:27,904 --> 00:46:29,564
[SPEAKER_02]: If the mind is aware, it will be at peace.

646
00:46:29,684 --> 00:46:32,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, as the suttas say, by discerning the sign of the mind.

647
00:46:32,785 --> 00:46:35,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning the signs and characteristics and properties of the mind.

648
00:46:36,366 --> 00:46:40,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Unnecessary precondition for arising of the right view.

649
00:46:41,227 --> 00:46:42,787
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you develop the right view?

650
00:46:43,888 --> 00:46:45,428
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you know the way out of suffering?

651
00:46:46,028 --> 00:46:48,329
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't even see your mind as a thing that's there.

652
00:46:48,589 --> 00:46:49,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is there, but you don't see it.

653
00:46:50,906 --> 00:46:52,968
[SPEAKER_00]: And how can you see it if you never make the effort to see it?

654
00:46:53,348 --> 00:47:04,136
[SPEAKER_00]: If all of your efforts are focused on the content of your mind, dwelling on the content, shutting the content down, being engaged fully with the content, you never step back to look at the context.

655
00:47:05,197 --> 00:47:09,200
[SPEAKER_00]: What's peripheral in regard to this content?

656
00:47:09,240 --> 00:47:12,623
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you never do that, you can never see what the mind is.

657
00:47:14,810 --> 00:47:18,133
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, there is absolutely no basis for understanding, no basis for the right view.

658
00:47:18,774 --> 00:47:20,836
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's literally, yeah, just going around in the circles.

659
00:47:23,198 --> 00:47:25,320
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if the mind is aware, it will be at peace.

660
00:47:26,221 --> 00:47:28,323
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people don't go for awareness.

661
00:47:28,923 --> 00:47:31,886
[SPEAKER_02]: They just want to have peace, a kind of blanking out.

662
00:47:32,106 --> 00:47:32,407
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

663
00:47:32,847 --> 00:47:34,028
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they never learn anything.

664
00:47:34,829 --> 00:47:40,014
[SPEAKER_02]: If we don't have this one who knows, what is there to base our practice on?

665
00:47:40,910 --> 00:47:43,511
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, even that one who knows, I heard many times, get mystified.

666
00:47:43,671 --> 00:47:43,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

667
00:47:44,071 --> 00:47:45,012
[SPEAKER_00]: The one who knows.

668
00:47:46,252 --> 00:47:47,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but do you know the one who knows?

669
00:47:48,273 --> 00:47:51,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you just mystifying your experience by ascribing it to the one who knows?

670
00:47:52,534 --> 00:47:53,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Call it whatever you want.

671
00:47:53,775 --> 00:47:54,655
[SPEAKER_00]: You must know it.

672
00:47:55,595 --> 00:47:57,676
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sotāpatti is knowing that you know.

673
00:47:58,336 --> 00:47:59,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just the one who knows.

674
00:48:00,457 --> 00:48:03,098
[SPEAKER_00]: That I took on trust of whatever mystified experience I had.

675
00:48:04,479 --> 00:48:05,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the one who knows, sure.

676
00:48:05,939 --> 00:48:06,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Call it that way.

677
00:48:07,060 --> 00:48:07,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you know him?

678
00:48:08,855 --> 00:48:09,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you fully understand it?

679
00:48:10,416 --> 00:48:11,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see its origin?

680
00:48:11,917 --> 00:48:12,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see its cessation?

681
00:48:13,698 --> 00:48:14,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see the way out of it?

682
00:48:15,460 --> 00:48:15,600
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

683
00:48:16,120 --> 00:48:21,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You just go around saying, it's just this, it's just being here like this, the one who knows, the pure awareness.

684
00:48:22,764 --> 00:48:23,485
[SPEAKER_00]: What does that even mean?

685
00:48:24,566 --> 00:48:25,686
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't understood any of it.

686
00:48:26,047 --> 00:48:28,208
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all just literally like a blanket.

687
00:48:28,368 --> 00:48:31,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Just use it, keep repeating this, to cover everything up.

688
00:48:31,170 --> 00:48:31,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

689
00:48:31,570 --> 00:48:34,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when there is no problem to be seen, there is no problem.

690
00:48:36,454 --> 00:48:37,635
[SPEAKER_02]: A kind of blanking out.

691
00:48:37,935 --> 00:48:38,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

692
00:48:40,911 --> 00:48:41,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Continues.

693
00:48:42,052 --> 00:48:52,036
[SPEAKER_02]: In order to calm the mind and become aware of the perceiver of sense impressions, we must observe it, follow the one who knows, train the mind until it's pure.

694
00:48:52,837 --> 00:48:54,177
[SPEAKER_02]: How pure should you make it?

695
00:48:55,118 --> 00:48:59,960
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's really pure, the mind should be above both good and evil, above even purity.

696
00:49:00,460 --> 00:49:00,901
[SPEAKER_02]: It's finished.

697
00:49:01,161 --> 00:49:05,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Make it as pure as necessary for not being able to suffer.

698
00:49:07,033 --> 00:49:07,353
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

699
00:49:08,014 --> 00:49:15,403
[SPEAKER_00]: When you can't suffer, when you're not liable to possibility of suffering, you don't have to make you anymore pure, because that will be pure.

700
00:49:16,584 --> 00:49:17,986
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's when the practice is finished.

701
00:49:18,026 --> 00:49:18,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

702
00:49:21,225 --> 00:49:25,288
[SPEAKER_02]: What people call sitting in meditation is merely a temporary kind of peace.

703
00:49:25,909 --> 00:49:28,891
[SPEAKER_02]: But even in such a peace, there are experiences.

704
00:49:29,892 --> 00:49:36,337
[SPEAKER_02]: If an experience arises, there must be someone who knows it, who looks into it, investigates and examines it.

705
00:49:37,258 --> 00:49:41,581
[SPEAKER_02]: If the mind is simply blank, then it's... that's not so useful.

706
00:49:42,642 --> 00:49:46,145
[SPEAKER_02]: You may see some people who look very restrained and think they are...

707
00:49:46,505 --> 00:49:48,306
[SPEAKER_02]: But the real peace is not simply the peace

708
00:49:49,287 --> 00:49:54,849
[SPEAKER_02]: The peaceful mind is not the peace which says, may I be happy and never experience any suffering.

709
00:49:55,989 --> 00:50:01,731
[SPEAKER_02]: With this kind of peace, eventually, even the attainment of happiness becomes unsatisfying.

710
00:50:01,811 --> 00:50:02,712
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering results.

711
00:50:03,852 --> 00:50:08,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Only when you can make your mind beyond both happiness and suffering, will you find true peace.

712
00:50:09,474 --> 00:50:11,254
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the true peace.

713
00:50:11,274 --> 00:50:13,215
[SPEAKER_02]: This is the subject most people never study.

714
00:50:13,655 --> 00:50:14,976
[SPEAKER_02]: They never really see this one.

715
00:50:15,216 --> 00:50:18,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they never made the effort to see it.

716
00:50:20,220 --> 00:50:26,724
[SPEAKER_00]: They never made the effort of questioning your own views, questioning what you think peace is, whether that really is peace.

717
00:50:28,825 --> 00:50:34,788
[SPEAKER_00]: About the meditation that you find helpful, well, do I ever reference what truly helpful things are?

718
00:50:35,448 --> 00:50:35,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I know that?

719
00:50:36,009 --> 00:50:38,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I just go by what makes me feel good?

720
00:50:40,691 --> 00:50:42,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're gonna... you have to start somehow.

721
00:50:42,512 --> 00:50:43,533
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not gonna be perfect.

722
00:50:44,153 --> 00:50:46,034
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you never make the effort to understand it,

723
00:50:47,068 --> 00:50:51,491
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just going to be following further down the line of the same imperfection, building on top of it.

724
00:50:59,037 --> 00:51:11,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by questioning your own views, you will be able to see which ones are less wrong, which ones are less likely to be wrong.

725
00:51:13,107 --> 00:51:16,210
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, by questioning those furthers, you get to discern them further.

726
00:51:16,772 --> 00:51:18,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you abandon the wrong views.

727
00:51:18,513 --> 00:51:21,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by shutting them out, but by fully penetrating them.

728
00:51:23,675 --> 00:51:24,476
[SPEAKER_00]: They get undone.

729
00:51:28,177 --> 00:51:30,738
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, all those questions that this person is asking, you could see.

730
00:51:31,699 --> 00:51:34,700
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't ask a question that's not already rooted in some sort of a view.

731
00:51:35,381 --> 00:51:37,322
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize, what's my general situation?

732
00:51:37,842 --> 00:51:39,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I free from suffering or not?

733
00:51:39,563 --> 00:51:39,703
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

734
00:51:40,343 --> 00:51:41,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, I don't have the right view.

735
00:51:41,984 --> 00:51:44,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, all my questions that come, are not right.

736
00:51:46,050 --> 00:51:49,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all I can do is not make them as wrong.

737
00:51:50,114 --> 00:51:54,358
[SPEAKER_00]: And you make them really, really wrong when you take them for granted at face value as right questions.

738
00:51:54,538 --> 00:51:55,940
[SPEAKER_00]: When you don't question your questions.

739
00:51:58,242 --> 00:52:00,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he was asking, how do I develop peace?

740
00:52:00,364 --> 00:52:02,206
[SPEAKER_00]: As if he already knows what peace is.

741
00:52:02,926 --> 00:52:07,110
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were to know what peace is, what tranquility is, you would be peace and tranquil.

742
00:52:07,531 --> 00:52:08,652
[SPEAKER_00]: You would have the right view.

743
00:52:10,870 --> 00:52:23,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is good in a sense of... well, he's at least aiming at some sort of tranquility, but it's not good, because he's not seeing that his idea of peace and tranquility cannot be the right one, because if it were the right one, he wouldn't be asking the question in the first place.

744
00:52:23,576 --> 00:52:26,437
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you would ask, what is peace?

745
00:52:26,457 --> 00:52:27,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he would... exactly, yeah.

746
00:52:27,878 --> 00:52:28,998
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be even more accurate.

747
00:52:29,719 --> 00:52:33,840
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize, I have a notion of peace, but I'm a putujana, which means that notion cannot be right.

748
00:52:34,701 --> 00:52:38,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of denying that, out of conceit or whatever, he admits it.

749
00:52:39,760 --> 00:52:42,184
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, he can ask, what is peace?

750
00:52:43,305 --> 00:52:44,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask somebody who he thinks he knows.

751
00:52:44,828 --> 00:52:46,190
[SPEAKER_00]: In this case, it was Ajahn Chah.

752
00:52:52,594 --> 00:53:02,645
[SPEAKER_00]: And if people start thinking like that, then the contradiction that your own thoughts are carrying in your day life, day practice, will become more and more apparent, and you won't be able to entertain them.

753
00:53:02,705 --> 00:53:03,466
[SPEAKER_00]: You will undo them.

754
00:53:04,026 --> 00:53:14,818
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't even question this, then you would be having things... saying things like, you know, the one who knows the nowhere, and completely mystify it, without any understanding of the nature of that.

755
00:53:17,478 --> 00:53:25,002
[SPEAKER_00]: And then just use this mystical kind of buzzword terminology to sort of glorify the present moment.

756
00:53:25,862 --> 00:53:27,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, be in the present moment.

757
00:53:28,604 --> 00:53:29,284
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not enough.

758
00:53:29,644 --> 00:53:32,386
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to understand the nature of it.

759
00:53:32,406 --> 00:53:37,768
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you start understanding, then you realize, as I said many times, being in the present moment is not an optional thing.

760
00:53:37,788 --> 00:53:38,949
[SPEAKER_00]: You're always in the present moment.

761
00:53:40,830 --> 00:53:42,551
[SPEAKER_00]: When you think about your past, you think it presently.

762
00:53:42,671 --> 00:53:44,332
[SPEAKER_00]: When you plan your future, you think it presently.

763
00:53:44,892 --> 00:53:46,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So, neither past nor future are the problem.

764
00:53:46,713 --> 00:53:47,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I must stop thinking about it.

765
00:53:48,394 --> 00:53:49,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't plan the future.

766
00:53:49,514 --> 00:53:50,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't think about the past.

767
00:53:50,615 --> 00:53:50,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Why not?

768
00:53:51,635 --> 00:53:52,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are just thoughts.

769
00:53:53,076 --> 00:53:54,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're blaming your thoughts for your suffering?

770
00:53:55,797 --> 00:53:58,958
[SPEAKER_00]: You're blaming your past for your suffering, or your future, which hasn't yet occurred?

771
00:54:00,019 --> 00:54:04,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means your suffering, then, cannot be undone by you, if it were truly in your past.

772
00:54:04,381 --> 00:54:05,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's done.

773
00:54:05,702 --> 00:54:06,902
[SPEAKER_00]: If it were truly in your future.

774
00:54:07,402 --> 00:54:08,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it cannot be done.

775
00:54:09,805 --> 00:54:10,745
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't, isn't it?

776
00:54:11,486 --> 00:54:13,307
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in your attitude in regard to your own thoughts.

777
00:54:16,769 --> 00:54:30,996
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you just take things for granted, like knowing you're a bhutujana, but then don't question even your own way of questioning, just assume that you already know things rightly, that's a contradiction in terms that you are not obviously aware of.

778
00:54:40,821 --> 00:54:56,572
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you develop stillness amidst the flow by discerning the context that remains immovable while its content is changing.

779
00:54:56,592 --> 00:55:00,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, one of the fundamental contexts is this body present here.

780
00:55:01,815 --> 00:55:03,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Your own body.

781
00:55:03,496 --> 00:55:06,578
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a basis of any other content of your experience.

782
00:55:07,379 --> 00:55:10,121
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't lose the sight of that context necessary basis,

783
00:55:11,033 --> 00:55:17,678
[SPEAKER_00]: A determination for any experience you have means you will not be overwhelmed or affected by any experience you have on account of your body.

784
00:55:18,199 --> 00:55:19,640
[SPEAKER_00]: And what experience you have on account of your body?

785
00:55:19,740 --> 00:55:21,542
[SPEAKER_00]: All experiences you have on account of your body.

786
00:55:23,443 --> 00:55:30,249
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would say, oh, he develops mindfulness of the body necessary to the extent necessary for final knowledge.

787
00:55:31,510 --> 00:55:34,032
[SPEAKER_00]: He developed the context of a necessary basis

788
00:55:35,972 --> 00:55:46,740
[SPEAKER_00]: For other things that are simultaneously present, on top of that basis, to the extent necessary for the mind to be completely free from ignorance and liability to suffer.

789
00:55:53,845 --> 00:55:58,108
[SPEAKER_00]: A necessary basis is what saṅkhara is.

790
00:56:00,390 --> 00:56:01,931
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he fully relinquished saṅkharas.

791
00:56:03,852 --> 00:56:06,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Extinguishing of sankhāras means extinguishing of sākharas.

792
00:56:08,597 --> 00:56:15,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Extinguishing of the ownership of the confusion of the ignorance of the necessary basis.

793
00:56:15,224 --> 00:56:17,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't remove the necessary basis structurally.

794
00:56:17,286 --> 00:56:18,127
[SPEAKER_00]: The body is still there.

795
00:56:18,848 --> 00:56:21,490
[SPEAKER_00]: But removes any affection that can arise on account of it.

796
00:56:21,851 --> 00:56:26,536
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why for an arahant, what's left is this body here and all the name and form externally.

797
00:56:31,521 --> 00:56:45,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no amount of change in the name and form, change in the elements, no amount of flow will disturb the fully developed, transcended, pretty much, extinguished ownership of that fundamental context.

798
00:56:46,070 --> 00:56:51,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if your experience is rooted necessarily in the presence of the body here, that is its necessary basis.

799
00:56:52,574 --> 00:56:54,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Your ownership is redundant.

800
00:56:54,535 --> 00:56:55,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It's secondary.

801
00:56:55,096 --> 00:56:56,076
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not its creator.

802
00:56:57,417 --> 00:56:58,778
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not its manager either.

803
00:56:59,739 --> 00:57:08,549
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only assume you're the owner, you are your own, because things are given to you beforehand, on their own.

804
00:57:10,031 --> 00:57:15,457
[SPEAKER_00]: But you recognize that the experience is there, because the body, liable to experience, is simultaneously there.

805
00:57:16,157 --> 00:57:19,962
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no room for the sense of self there, to be that owner and creator of the experience.

806
00:57:21,812 --> 00:57:22,772
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you disown it.

807
00:57:22,812 --> 00:57:36,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Not by denying the experience or denying the sense of self, but by simply realizing that it's impossible to own it, because even your ownership is on the necessary basis of this random body living here, being present now, with the beating heart and breathing lungs.

808
00:57:36,358 --> 00:57:37,178
[SPEAKER_00]: That you have no say in.

809
00:57:38,639 --> 00:57:42,180
[SPEAKER_00]: If those things you have no say in change, no amount of your say can make a difference.

810
00:57:42,820 --> 00:57:46,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize, my own say is not my own, fundamentally.

811
00:57:47,506 --> 00:57:51,768
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't realize that accidentally, because it's completely against the entire grain of your experience.

812
00:57:52,608 --> 00:57:55,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is going with that say, with that intention upon intention.

813
00:57:56,790 --> 00:58:10,836
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to keep making that effort to discern that context behind, and you discern it to the extent necessary, then you have fully uprooted any dependence, any liability to the content of that very context.

814
00:58:10,876 --> 00:58:12,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Context of impermanence.

815
00:58:12,837 --> 00:58:13,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Context of suffering.

816
00:58:21,103 --> 00:58:21,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

817
00:58:21,303 --> 00:58:21,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

818
00:58:21,965 --> 00:58:22,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

